/* Fun facts for the home page (Physics). Indonesian versions, in the same order, are in src/lang/id/facts.js. */
const FACTS = [
  { t: `Einstein's miracle year`, b: `In 1905, while working as a clerk at the patent office in Bern, the 26-year-old Albert Einstein published four papers that changed physics: on the photoelectric effect, on Brownian motion, on special relativity, and the short paper that contains E = mc². His 1921 Nobel Prize was awarded for the photoelectric effect, not for relativity.` },
  { t: `Your phone's GPS needs relativity`, b: `GPS satellites carry atomic clocks. Because they move fast, special relativity makes them tick slower by about 7 microseconds a day; because gravity is weaker up there, general relativity makes them tick faster by about 45 microseconds. The net 38 microseconds per day must be corrected, or positions would drift by roughly 10 km every day.` },
  { t: `Sunlight is 8 minutes old`, b: `Light travels at 299,792,458 metres per second, and it still needs about 8 minutes 20 seconds to cross the 150 million km from the Sun to the Earth. So you always see the Sun as it was a few minutes ago. Light from the nearest star beyond the Sun, Proxima Centauri, set off more than four years ago.` },
  { t: `The metre is defined by light`, b: `Since 1983 the speed of light in a vacuum has been fixed at exactly 299,792,458 m/s, and the metre is defined as the distance light travels in 1/299,792,458 of a second. Before that, the metre was a platinum-iridium bar kept near Paris, and originally it was meant to be one ten-millionth of the distance from the North Pole to the equator.` },
  { t: `The kilogram retired its metal cylinder`, b: `For 130 years the kilogram was defined by a single metal cylinder, "Le Grand K", locked in a vault near Paris. Copies around the world slowly drifted by tens of micrograms compared with it. Since May 2019 the kilogram has been defined by fixing the value of the Planck constant, so it can be realised anywhere with the right equipment.` },
  { t: `Hearing two black holes collide`, b: `On 14 September 2015 the LIGO detectors in the USA recorded gravitational waves for the first time, from two black holes that merged 1.3 billion light-years away. The wave changed the length of LIGO's 4 km arms by only a few thousandths of the width of a proton. Einstein had predicted such waves 100 years earlier, and the discovery won the 2017 Nobel Prize in Physics.` },
  { t: `The first photo of a black hole`, b: `In April 2019 the Event Horizon Telescope, a network of radio telescopes across the Earth acting like one planet-sized dish, released the first image of a black hole: the glowing ring around the giant black hole in the galaxy M87, 55 million light-years away and 6.5 billion times the mass of the Sun. In 2022 it imaged Sagittarius A*, the black hole at the centre of our own galaxy.` },
  { t: `The particle that gives mass`, b: `In 1964 Peter Higgs and others predicted a field that gives elementary particles their mass. Its particle, the Higgs boson, was finally found in 2012 at CERN's Large Hadron Collider, a 27 km ring buried under the French–Swiss border. Higgs and François Englert shared the 2013 Nobel Prize in Physics.` },
  { t: `Trillions of ghost particles pass through you`, b: `The Sun's fusion reactions produce huge numbers of neutrinos, tiny particles that hardly interact with anything. About 65 billion of them pass through every square centimetre of your body each second, day and night, because they go straight through the Earth. A neutrino could cross a light-year of lead with a good chance of not hitting anything.` },
  { t: `A teaspoon of neutron star`, b: `When a large star collapses, its core can become a neutron star: more mass than the Sun squeezed into a ball about 20 km across. The matter is so dense that one teaspoon of it would weigh billions of tonnes on Earth. Some neutron stars spin hundreds of times per second and sweep beams of radio waves across space like lighthouses; we call them pulsars.` },
  { t: `Electricity with zero resistance`, b: `In 1911 Heike Kamerlingh Onnes cooled mercury to 4.2 K (about −269 °C) and found that its electrical resistance suddenly vanished completely. This superconductivity lets a current flow in a loop for years without a power source. Hospital MRI scanners use superconducting coils cooled by liquid helium to make their strong magnetic fields.` },
  { t: `Colder than outer space`, b: `Absolute zero, 0 K or −273.15 °C, can be approached but never reached. Space between the galaxies is about 2.7 K, but laboratories are far colder: in 1995 Eric Cornell and Carl Wieman cooled atoms to billionths of a kelvin and made a Bose–Einstein condensate, where thousands of atoms behave as a single quantum object. They won the 2001 Nobel Prize.` },
  { t: `The hottest temperature ever made`, b: `In 2012 physicists at CERN's Large Hadron Collider smashed lead nuclei together and briefly created a quark–gluon plasma at around 5.5 trillion kelvin, recognised as the hottest human-made temperature. It is hundreds of thousands of times hotter than the centre of the Sun and recreates conditions from a millionth of a second after the Big Bang.` },
  { t: `Lightning is hotter than the Sun's surface`, b: `A lightning channel heats the air to around 30,000 °C in a few millionths of a second, about five times hotter than the visible surface of the Sun. The air expands so violently that it makes a shock wave, which we hear as thunder. Worldwide, lightning strikes somewhere about 40 to 50 times every second.` },
  { t: `Count the seconds after lightning`, b: `Light from a lightning flash reaches you almost instantly, but sound travels only about 343 m/s in air. Count the seconds between the flash and the thunder and divide by 3 to get the distance in kilometres. If the gap is less than about 30 seconds, the storm is within 10 km and close enough to strike you.` },
  { t: `The Moon is moving away`, b: `Apollo astronauts left mirrors on the Moon, and scientists still bounce laser pulses off them. The timing shows that the Moon is drifting away from Earth by about 3.8 cm every year, roughly as fast as your fingernails grow. The same tidal friction is slowly lengthening Earth's day by around 2 milliseconds per century.` },
  { t: `A hammer and a feather on the Moon`, b: `Galileo argued that without air resistance all objects fall at the same rate. In 1971 astronaut David Scott tested it on live television during Apollo 15: he dropped a geological hammer and a falcon feather on the airless Moon, and they hit the ground at the same moment. The story that Galileo dropped balls from the Leaning Tower of Pisa is probably a legend.` },
  { t: `Galileo found moons around Jupiter`, b: `In January 1610 Galileo pointed his homemade telescope at Jupiter and saw four small "stars" that changed position night after night. They were moons orbiting Jupiter, proof that not everything circles the Earth. Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto are still called the Galilean moons, and Ganymede is bigger than the planet Mercury.` },
  { t: `Eureka!`, b: `According to a story told by the Roman writer Vitruvius, Archimedes was asked to find out whether a king's crown was pure gold. Noticing the water rise as he got into his bath, he realised he could measure the crown's volume by the water it displaced, and ran through the streets shouting "Eureka!" ("I have found it!"). His principle of buoyancy explains why ships float.` },
  { t: `Radioactivity was found in a drawer`, b: `In 1896 Henri Becquerel planned to test whether uranium salts glow after sunlight. The weather was cloudy, so he put the salts and a wrapped photographic plate in a drawer. When he developed the plate anyway, it was fogged: the uranium gave off radiation on its own. He shared the 1903 Nobel Prize with Marie and Pierre Curie.` },
  { t: `The first X-ray showed a wedding ring`, b: `In 1895 Wilhelm Röntgen discovered invisible rays that pass through flesh but not bone. One of his first X-ray pictures showed the bones of his wife Anna Bertha's hand with her wedding ring. He called them X-rays because "X" stands for the unknown, and he received the very first Nobel Prize in Physics in 1901.` },
  { t: `A melted chocolate bar led to the microwave oven`, b: `In 1945 the engineer Percy Spencer was working on radar equipment when he noticed that a chocolate bar in his pocket had melted. He tried popcorn kernels next, and they popped. Microwaves make water molecules in food rotate back and forth billions of times a second, and that motion heats the food.` },
  { t: `Billions of switches in your pocket`, b: `The transistor was invented at Bell Labs in 1947 by John Bardeen, Walter Brattain and William Shockley. Today a single phone or computer chip contains billions of transistors, each only a few tens of atoms across in its smallest features. Bardeen later won a second Nobel Prize in Physics for explaining superconductivity, the only person to win that prize twice.` },
  { t: `The Sun loses 4 million tonnes a second`, b: `The Sun shines by fusing hydrogen into helium in its core. Each second it turns about 4 million tonnes of matter into energy, exactly as E = mc² says. Even at that rate, it has enough hydrogen to keep shining for about another 5 billion years.` },
  { t: `Quantum tunnelling keeps the Sun burning`, b: `Protons in the Sun's core repel each other so strongly that, by classical physics, they should almost never get close enough to fuse. Quantum mechanics lets them "tunnel" through the barrier with a tiny probability, and with so many protons that is enough to power the Sun. The same tunnelling effect is used to store data in the flash memory of USB drives and phones.` },
  { t: `One hour of sunlight could power the world for a year`, b: `The sunlight reaching the Earth delivers about 170,000 terawatts. Humanity uses energy at an average rate of roughly 20 terawatts, so the Earth receives as much solar energy in about an hour as people use in a whole year. The challenge of solar power is not the amount of sunlight but collecting and storing it cheaply.` },
  { t: `Rainbows are really circles`, b: `A rainbow forms when sunlight is refracted, reflected inside raindrops and refracted again, leaving at about 42° from the point directly opposite the Sun. All drops at that angle form a circle around that point. From the ground the horizon cuts it off, but from an aeroplane you can sometimes see a complete circular rainbow.` },
  { t: `Why the sky is blue and sunsets are red`, b: `Air molecules scatter short-wavelength blue light much more strongly than red light, an effect explained by Lord Rayleigh in 1871. Blue light is scattered all over the sky, so the sky looks blue. At sunset, sunlight crosses much more air, most of the blue is scattered away before it reaches you, and the Sun and clouds look orange and red.` },
  { t: `Ice floats, and that saves lake life`, b: `Most substances are denser as solids, but water expands by about 9% when it freezes, and it is densest at about 4 °C. So ice floats and a frozen lake freezes from the top down. The ice layer insulates the water below, which stays liquid at around 4 °C and lets fish survive the winter.` },
  { t: `Water droplets that dance on a hot pan`, b: `Drop water on a pan much hotter than 100 °C and the drops skate around instead of boiling away at once. The bottom of each drop instantly turns to vapour, and this thin cushion of steam insulates the rest of the drop. This is the Leidenfrost effect, described in 1756, and it is why cooks test a hot pan by sprinkling water on it.` },
  { t: `Galaxies are rushing away from us`, b: `In the 1920s Edwin Hubble, using work by Henrietta Leavitt and Vesto Slipher, found that the light from distant galaxies is shifted toward red, and the farther the galaxy, the larger the shift. This Doppler-like redshift showed that the universe is expanding, the first strong evidence for what became the Big Bang theory.` },
  { t: `The echo of the Big Bang was blamed on pigeons`, b: `In 1964 Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson could not get rid of a faint hiss in their radio antenna in New Jersey. They even cleaned out pigeon droppings, but the signal came equally from every direction. It was the cosmic microwave background, the afterglow of the Big Bang, and it earned them the 1978 Nobel Prize. A small part of the "snow" on old analogue TVs came from the same radiation.` },
  { t: `Voyager 1, the farthest human-made object`, b: `Launched in 1977, Voyager 1 flew past Jupiter and Saturn and in 2012 became the first spacecraft to enter interstellar space. It is now more than 24 billion km from Earth, so its radio signals, travelling at the speed of light, take over 22 hours to reach us. It carries a golden record with sounds and music from Earth.` },
  { t: `Sixteen sunrises a day`, b: `The International Space Station orbits about 400 km above the Earth at roughly 28,000 km/h, going round the planet about every 90 minutes. That means the astronauts on board see about 16 sunrises and 16 sunsets every day. Up there, gravity is still about 90% as strong as on the ground; astronauts float because they are falling around the Earth continuously.` },
  { t: `Escape velocity`, b: `To leave the Earth without further pushing, an object would need to be launched at about 11.2 km/s, over 40,000 km/h. That is the escape velocity. Rockets do not need to reach it instantly because their engines keep pushing, but spacecraft heading to the Moon or planets must eventually reach speeds close to it.` },
  { t: `The war of the currents`, b: `In the 1880s Thomas Edison promoted direct current (DC), while Nikola Tesla and George Westinghouse backed alternating current (AC). AC won because transformers can easily raise its voltage for long-distance transmission, which wastes much less energy, and lower it again for homes. Almost all power grids today use AC, at 50 or 60 Hz.` },
  { t: `Faraday, the bookbinder's apprentice`, b: `Michael Faraday had only basic schooling and at 14 became a bookbinder's apprentice, reading the science books he bound. He went on to discover electromagnetic induction in 1831, the principle behind every electric generator and transformer. He had little mathematics, so he pictured "lines of force"; the unit of capacitance, the farad, is named after him.` },
  { t: `Maxwell discovered what light is`, b: `In the 1860s James Clerk Maxwell combined the laws of electricity and magnetism into a set of equations. They predicted waves of electric and magnetic fields travelling at exactly the measured speed of light, so he concluded that light itself is an electromagnetic wave. In 1887 Heinrich Hertz produced such waves in his lab, the first radio waves made on purpose.` },
  { t: `A compass needle during a lecture`, b: `In 1820 the Danish physicist Hans Christian Ørsted noticed during a lecture that a compass needle moved whenever he switched on an electric current in a nearby wire. It was the first evidence that electricity and magnetism are connected. Within months, André-Marie Ampère had worked out the laws of the magnetic force between currents.` },
  { t: `A film star invented part of Wi-Fi`, b: `In 1942 the Hollywood actress Hedy Lamarr and the composer George Antheil patented a "frequency hopping" system to stop enemies jamming radio-controlled torpedoes. The signal jumps between frequencies in a pattern known only to sender and receiver. Similar spread-spectrum ideas are used today in Bluetooth, GPS and some forms of Wi-Fi.` },
  { t: `A pendulum proved the Earth turns`, b: `In 1851 Léon Foucault hung a 28 kg ball on a 67 m wire from the dome of the Panthéon in Paris. As it swung, its direction of swing slowly turned around, because the Earth was rotating beneath it. It was the first simple, visible demonstration of the Earth's rotation, and Foucault pendulums now swing in science museums around the world.` },
  { t: `The first nuclear reactor was under a stadium`, b: `On 2 December 1942 Enrico Fermi's team started the first human-made nuclear chain reaction in Chicago Pile-1, a stack of graphite blocks and uranium built in a squash court under the stands of the University of Chicago's football stadium. It produced about half a watt, less than a torch bulb, but it opened the nuclear age.` },
  { t: `Fusion finally gave more energy than it took`, b: `In December 2022 the National Ignition Facility in California fired 192 lasers carrying 2.05 megajoules at a peppercorn-sized fuel capsule and got 3.15 megajoules of fusion energy out. It was the first time a fusion experiment produced more energy than the laser energy delivered to the fuel, a milestone scientists had chased for 60 years.` },
  { t: `The blue glow of nuclear reactors`, b: `Nothing can travel faster than light in a vacuum, but light slows to about 75% of that speed in water. Electrons from a reactor core can move faster than light does in water, and they give off a blue glow called Cherenkov radiation, a kind of optical "sonic boom". Pavel Cherenkov shared the 1958 Nobel Prize for explaining it.` },
  { t: `Light slowed to bicycle speed`, b: `In 1999 the Danish physicist Lene Hau sent a laser pulse through an ultracold cloud of sodium atoms, a Bose–Einstein condensate, and slowed it to about 17 metres per second, roughly the speed of a fast cyclist. Two years later her team stopped a light pulse completely and then released it again.` },
  { t: `An equation on a tombstone`, b: `Ludwig Boltzmann explained entropy by counting the number of ways W that the atoms of a system can be arranged: S = k log W. Many scientists of his time doubted that atoms were real, and he died in 1906. The equation is engraved on his tombstone in Vienna, and the constant k is now named after him.` },
  { t: `Schrödinger's cat was meant as a joke`, b: `In 1935 Erwin Schrödinger imagined a cat in a sealed box whose life depends on a random radioactive decay. According to quantum mechanics, until someone looks the system is in a mixture of both outcomes, so the cat would be "both alive and dead". He meant it to show how strange that view was, but the cat became the most famous image of quantum physics.` },
  { t: `You cannot know everything at once`, b: `Werner Heisenberg's uncertainty principle (1927) says that the more precisely you know a particle's position, the less precisely you can know its momentum, and vice versa. It is not a problem with our instruments but a basic property of nature. It explains why electrons in atoms do not simply fall into the nucleus.` },
  { t: `The Earth's magnetic field flips`, b: `The Earth's magnetic north and south poles have swapped places hundreds of times in its history. We know because volcanic rocks record the direction of the field when they cool, leaving magnetic "stripes" on the ocean floor. The last full reversal happened about 780,000 years ago, and the magnetic north pole is currently drifting by tens of kilometres a year.` },
  { t: `Auroras are the solar wind hitting our air`, b: `The Sun blows out a stream of charged particles, the solar wind. The Earth's magnetic field steers some of them toward the poles, where they crash into oxygen and nitrogen high in the atmosphere and make them glow: green and red from oxygen, blue and purple from nitrogen. During strong solar storms, auroras can be seen much closer to the equator than usual.` },
  { t: `Insects that walk on water`, b: `Water striders can stand and run on the surface of a pond. Water molecules attract each other strongly, creating surface tension that acts like a stretched skin. The insects' legs are covered in tiny water-repellent hairs, so they only dent the surface instead of breaking through it. Surface tension also makes raindrops round and lets a steel needle float if you lay it down gently.` },
  { t: `Old window glass does not flow`, b: `It is often said that old church windows are thicker at the bottom because glass is a very slow liquid. In fact, medieval glassmaking produced uneven panes, and builders often put the thick edge at the bottom. Calculations show that at room temperature glass would take far longer than the age of the universe to sag noticeably.` },
  { t: `The loudest sound in recorded history`, b: `When Krakatoa in the Sunda Strait erupted on 27 August 1883, the explosion was heard on Rodrigues Island near Mauritius, almost 4,800 km away. Barometers around the world recorded its pressure wave passing several times as it travelled round and round the planet. The eruption and its tsunamis killed more than 36,000 people.` },
  { t: `Why you are safe in a car during lightning`, b: `A car's metal body acts as a Faraday cage: electric charge from a lightning strike flows around the outside of the metal shell and into the ground, leaving the inside almost unaffected. It is the metal, not the rubber tyres, that protects you. The same principle shields sensitive electronics and explains why your phone loses signal in a lift.` },
  { t: `Orchestras tune to 440 Hz`, b: `The note A above middle C is standardised at 440 vibrations per second, which is why an orchestra tunes to the oboe's A before a concert. An octave higher doubles the frequency to 880 Hz. Human hearing covers roughly 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz, and the upper limit drops steadily with age.` },
  { t: `A dropped bullet and a fired bullet land together`, b: `If you fire a bullet horizontally and drop another from the same height at the same instant, both hit flat ground at the same time (ignoring air resistance). Horizontal motion does not affect vertical falling; gravity pulls both down equally. This independence of horizontal and vertical motion is the key idea behind projectile motion.` },
  { t: `You would weigh one sixth on the Moon`, b: `The Moon's surface gravity is about 1.6 m/s², roughly one sixth of Earth's 9.8 m/s². Your mass stays the same, but your weight, the force of gravity on you, drops to a sixth. That is why Apollo astronauts could bound across the surface in heavy suits and backpacks weighing around 90 kg on Earth.` },
  { t: `Breaking the sound barrier`, b: `Sound travels at about 343 m/s (1,235 km/h) in air at 20 °C. On 14 October 1947 Chuck Yeager flew the rocket plane Bell X-1 faster than sound for the first time. An object going faster than sound pushes air into a shock wave that we hear on the ground as a sonic boom. The crack of a whip is a small sonic boom made by its tip.` },
  { t: `Newton's Principia`, b: `In 1687 Isaac Newton published the Principia, which set out his three laws of motion and the law of universal gravitation. It showed that the same force that makes an apple fall keeps the Moon in orbit and the planets moving round the Sun. The book was paid for by the astronomer Edmond Halley, of Halley's comet.` },
  { t: `Marie Curie's notebooks are still radioactive`, b: `Marie Curie discovered polonium and radium and won Nobel Prizes in both physics (1903) and chemistry (1911), the only person to win in two different sciences. Her notebooks are still so radioactive that they are kept in lead-lined boxes in Paris, and visitors must sign a waiver and wear protective clothing to consult them.` },
  { t: `Atoms are almost entirely empty`, b: `In 1909–1911 Ernest Rutherford's team fired alpha particles at thin gold foil. Most went straight through, but a few bounced back, which Rutherford compared to firing a shell at tissue paper and having it come back at you. He concluded that almost all of an atom's mass is in a tiny nucleus about 100,000 times smaller than the atom itself.` },
  { t: `Light is a wave and a particle`, b: `Thomas Young's double-slit experiment in 1801 showed light making interference stripes like water waves. But in 1905 Einstein explained the photoelectric effect by treating light as particles, photons. Modern experiments send single photons, or even single electrons, through two slits one at a time, and they still build up an interference pattern.` },
  { t: `Why a spinning skater speeds up`, b: `When a figure skater pulls in her arms during a spin, she spins much faster. Her angular momentum stays constant, and pulling mass closer to the axis reduces her moment of inertia, so her rotation speed must rise. The same law explains why a collapsing star can become a pulsar spinning hundreds of times a second.` },
  { t: `Why ships made of steel float`, b: `Steel is almost eight times denser than water, yet huge steel ships float. A ship's hull encloses a large volume of air, so the average density of the ship is less than that of water. It sinks until the water it pushes aside weighs as much as the whole ship, exactly as Archimedes' principle predicts.` },
  { t: `Why the Moon always shows the same face`, b: `The Moon turns on its axis exactly once for every orbit around the Earth, about every 27.3 days, so the same side always faces us. This "tidal locking" happened because the Earth's gravity raised bulges on the young Moon and slowly braked its spin. We only saw the far side in 1959, in photos from the Soviet probe Luna 3.` },
  { t: `You are travelling very fast right now`, b: `Sitting still in Indonesia, near the equator, the Earth's rotation carries you east at about 1,670 km/h. The Earth orbits the Sun at about 107,000 km/h, and the Sun carries the whole Solar System around the centre of the Milky Way at roughly 800,000 km/h. We do not feel it because the motion is almost perfectly steady.` },
  { t: `Seasons are not caused by distance from the Sun`, b: `The Earth is actually closest to the Sun in early January, during winter in the Northern Hemisphere. Seasons happen because the Earth's axis is tilted by about 23.4°: for half the year one hemisphere leans towards the Sun, gets higher sunshine and longer days. Near the equator the tilt matters little, which is why Indonesia has wet and dry seasons instead.` },
  { t: `A day on Venus is longer than its year`, b: `Venus spins so slowly, and backwards, that one rotation takes about 243 Earth days, longer than its 225-day orbit around the Sun. Its thick carbon dioxide atmosphere traps so much heat that the surface is about 465 °C, hotter than Mercury, even though Mercury is closer to the Sun.` },
  { t: `Saturn would float in water`, b: `Saturn is mostly hydrogen and helium, and its average density is only about 0.69 g/cm³, less than that of water. If you could find a bathtub big enough, it would float. Its famous rings are made of countless pieces of ice and rock, yet they are mostly only about 10 metres thick across hundreds of thousands of kilometres.` },
  { t: `Light from stars is old news`, b: `When you look at the star Betelgeuse, you see light that left it roughly 500 to 700 years ago. The Andromeda Galaxy, just visible to the naked eye on dark nights, is about 2.5 million light-years away, so its light set out when early humans were beginning to make stone tools. Looking far into space means looking back in time.` },
  { t: `The James Webb Space Telescope sees the infant universe`, b: `Launched on 25 December 2021, the James Webb Space Telescope has a 6.5 m gold-coated mirror made of 18 hexagonal segments and a sunshield the size of a tennis court. It works in infrared at about −233 °C, 1.5 million km from Earth, and has photographed galaxies whose light left them only a few hundred million years after the Big Bang.` },
  { t: `Most of the universe is invisible`, b: `Ordinary matter, everything made of atoms, makes up only about 5% of the energy content of the universe. About 27% is dark matter, detected only by its gravity on galaxies, and about 68% is dark energy, which is making the expansion of the universe speed up. The discovery of that acceleration in 1998 earned the 2011 Nobel Prize in Physics.` },
  { t: `Vera Rubin and dark matter`, b: `In the 1970s the American astronomer Vera Rubin measured how fast stars orbit in spiral galaxies and found that the outer stars move far too fast to be held by the visible matter alone. Her measurements were among the strongest early evidence for dark matter. The Vera C. Rubin Observatory in Chile, which began surveying the sky in 2025, is named after her.` },
  { t: `A ship of bubbles`, b: `Air bubbles can make water much less dense, which is why objects sink more easily in foamy water; this is one theory for ships lost in areas where gas escapes from the sea floor. Engineers use the same idea the other way: some ships pump a carpet of air bubbles under the hull to cut drag and save fuel.` },
  { t: `Why a bicycle does not fall over`, b: `A moving bicycle stays upright mostly because of steering: when it starts to lean, the front wheel turns into the lean and brings the wheels back under the rider. Scientists showed in 2011 that neither the gyroscopic effect nor the "trail" of the front fork is essential; a bicycle's self-stability depends on the way its mass and geometry are arranged.` },
  { t: `Why ice is slippery`, b: `Ice is slippery even well below freezing, and it was long thought that pressure from skates melts it. That effect is far too small. Modern research shows the surface of ice has a thin, disordered layer of water molecules that are only loosely held, and friction heating adds a little more, letting blades and shoes glide.` },
  { t: `Hot water can freeze faster than cold`, b: `In 1963 the Tanzanian schoolboy Erasto Mpemba noticed that hot ice-cream mix froze faster than cold mix in his school freezer. The "Mpemba effect" has been reported since Aristotle, but it is hard to reproduce and depends on evaporation, convection and dissolved gases. Physicists still debate when, and whether, it really happens with water.` },
  { t: `The Coriolis effect and your sink`, b: `It is a myth that sinks drain clockwise in one hemisphere and anticlockwise in the other. The Coriolis force from the Earth's rotation is far too weak at that small scale; the swirl comes from the shape of the basin and how the water was poured. But on the scale of weather systems it dominates, making cyclones spin in opposite directions on either side of the equator.` },
  { t: `Your body runs on about 100 watts`, b: `An adult at rest uses energy at roughly 80 to 100 watts, about the same as an old light bulb, and the brain alone uses about 20 watts. Food energy is measured in kilocalories: 2,000 kcal a day is about 8.4 megajoules, which is roughly 97 watts averaged over 24 hours.` },
  { t: `Lightning strikes the same place twice`, b: `The saying is wrong: tall, pointed, well-grounded objects are struck again and again. New York's Empire State Building is hit about 20 to 25 times a year. That is why lightning rods, invented by Benjamin Franklin in the 1750s, work: they give the discharge a safe path to the ground.` },
  { t: `Birds on power lines are safe`, b: `A bird sitting on a single high-voltage wire does not get a shock because both of its feet are at almost the same voltage, so almost no current flows through it. The danger comes if a bird, or a person, touches two wires, or a wire and something grounded, at the same time. That is why large birds such as eagles are sometimes electrocuted on power poles.` },
  { t: `The first law of thermodynamics came from a doctor`, b: `In 1842 the German ship's doctor Julius Robert Mayer noticed that sailors' blood in the tropics was brighter red, and reasoned that the body needed to burn less fuel to stay warm. This led him to propose that heat and work are forms of the same energy. James Joule measured the exchange rate precisely with a paddle wheel stirring water.` },
  { t: `The perpetual motion machine is impossible`, b: `For centuries inventors designed wheels and pumps that would run forever without an energy source. All fail, because the first law of thermodynamics forbids creating energy and the second forbids turning heat completely into work. Since 1911 the United States patent office has asked for a working model before it will consider such an application.` },
  { t: `Why pressure cookers cook faster`, b: `Water boils at 100 °C only at normal air pressure. A pressure cooker traps the steam, raising the pressure to about twice atmospheric, so the water boils at about 120 °C and food cooks much faster. On a high mountain the opposite happens: on Everest's summit water boils at only about 70 °C, too cool to cook rice properly.` },
  { t: `Sound travels faster in water and steel`, b: `Sound moves at about 343 m/s in air, but about 1,480 m/s in water and around 5,900 m/s in steel. That is why whales can communicate over hundreds of kilometres of ocean, and why in old films someone puts an ear to a railway track to hear a distant train before it can be heard through the air.` },
  { t: `Bats and dolphins see with sound`, b: `Bats and dolphins send out high-pitched clicks and listen for the echoes to find prey and obstacles, a technique called echolocation. Some bats use frequencies above 100,000 Hz, far beyond human hearing, and can detect a wire thinner than a human hair. Ships' sonar and hospital ultrasound scanners use the same physics.` },
  { t: `An opera singer can shatter a glass`, b: `Every wine glass has a natural frequency at which it rings when tapped. If a singer holds exactly that note loudly enough, the glass vibrates more and more strongly, a phenomenon called resonance, until it can crack. Resonance also explains why soldiers break step when crossing bridges and why engineers design buildings to avoid shaking in time with earthquakes.` },
  { t: `The Tacoma Narrows Bridge danced itself apart`, b: `In November 1940, only four months after it opened, the Tacoma Narrows Bridge in the United States twisted violently in a steady 64 km/h wind and collapsed. The cause was aeroelastic flutter, in which the wind and the bridge's twisting fed energy into each other. Film of "Galloping Gertie" is still shown to engineering students everywhere.` },
  { t: `Why you see lightning before you hear thunder`, b: `Light covers a kilometre in about 3 millionths of a second, but sound needs about 3 seconds. Fireworks show the same thing: in a large stadium, people far from the launch site see the burst before they hear the bang. The ratio of the two speeds is almost a million.` },
  { t: `A magnet can levitate a train`, b: `Maglev trains float above the track using powerful magnets, so there is no wheel friction. The Shanghai maglev, opened in 2004, runs at up to 300 km/h in regular service, and in 2015 a Japanese test train reached 603 km/h, the world speed record for a train.` },
  { t: `Why metal feels colder than wood`, b: `A metal spoon and a wooden spoon left in the same room are at the same temperature, yet the metal feels colder. Metal conducts heat away from your fingers much faster, so your skin cools more quickly. Your sense of touch measures the flow of heat, not temperature itself.` },
  { t: `Thermos flasks stop all three kinds of heat flow`, b: `A vacuum flask, invented by James Dewar in 1892, keeps drinks hot or cold by blocking all three ways heat travels. The vacuum between two glass walls stops conduction and convection, and the silvered surfaces reflect radiation. The same idea is used to store liquid nitrogen and liquid helium in laboratories.` },
  { t: `The whole Internet travels on light`, b: `More than 95% of international data travels as pulses of infrared light through optical fibres, many laid on the sea floor. A single modern fibre pair can carry tens of terabits per second. The light stays inside by total internal reflection, and Charles Kao, who showed in 1966 that very pure glass could carry signals far, won the 2009 Nobel Prize in Physics.` },
  { t: `LEDs won a Nobel Prize`, b: `Red and green LEDs existed since the 1960s, but white LED lighting needed a bright blue LED, which proved extremely hard to make. Isamu Akasaki, Hiroshi Amano and Shuji Nakamura succeeded in the early 1990s using gallium nitride and shared the 2014 Nobel Prize in Physics. LED bulbs use about 80% less electricity than old filament bulbs.` },
  { t: `Touch screens use your body's electricity`, b: `Most phone screens are capacitive: a grid of transparent electrodes creates a small electric field, and your finger, which conducts electricity, changes the capacitance at the point you touch. That is why ordinary gloves do not work on phones, but gloves with conductive thread in the fingertips do.` },
  { t: `Satellites fall around the Earth`, b: `Newton imagined a cannon on a mountain firing faster and faster. At about 7.9 km/s the ball would fall towards the Earth as fast as the curved surface drops away beneath it, so it would never land: it would be in orbit. Every satellite is falling continuously; it just keeps missing the ground.` },
  { t: `Geostationary satellites hover over the equator`, b: `At a height of about 35,786 km above the equator, a satellite takes exactly one day to orbit, so it stays above the same spot on Earth. Television and weather satellites use this geostationary orbit, which was popularised by the science fiction writer Arthur C. Clarke in 1945. Indonesia's Palapa satellites, first launched in 1976, sit in this orbit.` },
  { t: `Tides happen twice a day`, b: `The Moon's gravity pulls hardest on the side of the Earth nearest to it and weakest on the far side, stretching the oceans into two bulges. As the Earth turns, most coasts pass through both bulges, giving two high tides a day about 12 hours 25 minutes apart. When the Sun and Moon line up at new and full moon, the tides are strongest: spring tides.` },
  { t: `A spoonful of the Sun's core`, b: `The core of the Sun is at about 15 million °C and is compressed to about 150 times the density of water, more than seven times denser than gold. Yet a cubic metre of it produces only about as much power as a compost heap, around 300 watts. The Sun shines so brightly only because its core is enormous.` },
  { t: `Sunlight takes thousands of years to leave the Sun`, b: `Energy made in the Sun's core does not fly straight out. Photons are absorbed and re-emitted countless times on a random walk through the dense interior, and estimates suggest the energy takes tens of thousands of years or more to reach the surface. From there, the light reaches us in just over 8 minutes.` },
  { t: `Every gold atom was made in space`, b: `Nuclear fusion in ordinary stars builds elements only up to iron. Heavier elements such as gold, silver and uranium form in the extreme bursts of neutrons released when neutron stars collide or massive stars explode as supernovae. The gold in a ring on your finger is older than the Earth.` },
  { t: `The Bose in boson came from India`, b: `In 1924 the Indian physicist Satyendra Nath Bose sent Einstein a paper deriving Planck's law of radiation in a new way. Einstein translated it into German, extended it, and together their work predicted the Bose–Einstein condensate. Particles such as photons that obey these statistics are called bosons in his honour.` },
  { t: `C. V. Raman won a Nobel Prize with homemade equipment`, b: `In 1928 the Indian physicist C. V. Raman discovered that a tiny fraction of light scattered by molecules changes colour, revealing the molecule's vibrations. The "Raman effect" earned him the 1930 Nobel Prize in Physics, the first for a scientist from Asia, and Raman spectroscopy is now used to identify substances from pigments to drugs.` },
  { t: `Chien-Shiung Wu showed nature is not mirror-symmetric`, b: `In 1956 the Chinese-American physicist Chien-Shiung Wu performed an experiment with radioactive cobalt-60 that showed the weak nuclear force distinguishes left from right. The theorists who proposed the idea, Lee and Yang, won the 1957 Nobel Prize; Wu, whose experiment proved it, did not, which many consider a great injustice.` },
  { t: `Lise Meitner explained nuclear fission`, b: `In 1938 Otto Hahn found barium when bombarding uranium with neutrons. His former colleague Lise Meitner, who had fled Nazi Germany, worked out with her nephew Otto Frisch that the uranium nucleus had split, and used E = mc² to calculate the energy released. Hahn alone received the Nobel Prize; element 109, meitnerium, now honours her.` },
  { t: `A cloud chamber shows invisible radiation`, b: `Fill a box with cold alcohol vapour and radioactive particles passing through leave trails of tiny droplets, like aircraft contrails. Charles Wilson invented the cloud chamber in 1911, and in 1932 Carl Anderson used one to discover the positron, the first known antimatter particle. You can see cosmic ray tracks in a homemade cloud chamber with dry ice.` },
  { t: `Antimatter is used in hospitals`, b: `When a particle of matter meets its antiparticle, both vanish in a flash of energy. PET scanners in hospitals use this: a patient receives a tracer that emits positrons, each positron meets an electron and produces two gamma rays flying in opposite directions, and detectors reconstruct where it happened to map activity in the body.` },
  { t: `Superfluid helium climbs walls`, b: `Cooled below about 2.17 K, liquid helium-4 becomes a superfluid with zero viscosity. It can flow through gaps too small for ordinary liquids and even creep up the walls of its container and over the rim as a thin film. Pyotr Kapitsa, John Allen and Don Misener discovered superfluidity in 1937.` },
  { t: `Quantum computers use superposition`, b: `An ordinary bit is 0 or 1, but a quantum bit (qubit) can be in a superposition of both. With many entangled qubits, quantum computers could solve certain problems, such as simulating molecules or factoring huge numbers, much faster than ordinary computers. The machines built so far still have too many errors for most practical tasks.` },
  { t: `Entanglement: "spooky action at a distance"`, b: `Two entangled particles share one quantum state: measuring one instantly tells you about the other, however far apart they are. Einstein called it "spooky action at a distance". Experiments by Alain Aspect, John Clauser and Anton Zeilinger confirmed it and won the 2022 Nobel Prize, although it cannot be used to send messages faster than light.` },
  { t: `Mass increases with speed? Not quite`, b: `It is often said that objects get heavier as they approach the speed of light. Modern physics prefers to say that mass stays the same but momentum and energy grow without limit, so pushing a spacecraft ever closer to c takes ever more energy. That is why nothing with mass can reach the speed of light.` },
  { t: `Muons prove time dilation every day`, b: `Cosmic rays create particles called muons about 15 km up in the atmosphere. Muons decay in about 2.2 millionths of a second, so even at nearly light speed they should travel only about 660 m. Yet many reach the ground, because their fast-moving clocks run slow as special relativity predicts. It was measured in 1941 and again, famously, in 1963.` },
  { t: `Clocks run faster on a mountain`, b: `General relativity says that time passes more slowly in stronger gravity. In 2010 physicists at NIST compared two atomic clocks whose heights differed by only 33 cm and measured the difference. Over a lifetime, someone living on a high mountain ages a tiny fraction of a millisecond faster than someone at sea level.` },
  { t: `The world's most precise clocks`, b: `Optical atomic clocks, which count the vibrations of light absorbed by atoms such as strontium, are so stable that they would lose less than one second over the age of the universe. They are precise enough to detect the change in gravity from lifting the clock by a centimetre, and may soon replace caesium clocks in the definition of the second.` },
  { t: `Stephen Hawking and black hole radiation`, b: `In 1974 Stephen Hawking showed that, because of quantum effects near the event horizon, black holes should slowly emit radiation and eventually evaporate. Hawking radiation has never been observed, because for real black holes it is extremely faint, but it linked gravity, quantum mechanics and thermodynamics in a new way.` },
  { t: `Indonesia's own space observatory`, b: `Indonesia's National Observatory at Timau, in East Nusa Tenggara, was built for its dark, dry skies and houses a 3.8-metre telescope, among the largest in Southeast Asia. The older Bosscha Observatory in Lembang, West Java, has observed double stars and the Sun since 1923, but city lights around Bandung have made its sky much brighter.` },
  { t: `Geothermal power: Indonesia's hidden heat`, b: `Indonesia sits on the Ring of Fire and holds some of the world's largest geothermal resources. Power plants such as Kamojang in West Java, operating since 1983, pipe natural steam from hot rock deep underground to spin turbines. Indonesia is the second-largest producer of geothermal electricity in the world, after the United States.` },
  { t: `A bullet train's nose was copied from a bird`, b: `Japan's early Shinkansen trains made a loud boom when they exited tunnels, because they pushed a pressure wave ahead of them. Engineer Eiji Nakatsu, a bird-watcher, redesigned the nose of the 500 Series (1997) after the long, narrow beak of the kingfisher, which dives into water with hardly a splash. The train became quieter, faster and used less energy.` },
  { t: `Newton and the plague year`, b: `In 1665–66 Cambridge University closed because of the plague, and the young Isaac Newton went home to Woolsthorpe. There he worked on calculus, split white light into colours with a prism and began thinking about gravity. The story of a falling apple comes from Newton himself, told in old age, though the apple probably did not hit his head.` },
  { t: `Weighing the Earth in a shed`, b: `In 1798 Henry Cavendish used a torsion balance, lead balls hung from a thin wire, to measure the tiny gravitational pull between them. From this he calculated that the Earth's average density is about 5.48 times that of water, very close to today's 5.51. His experiment is often called "weighing the Earth".` },
  { t: `Neptune was found with a pencil`, b: `Uranus was not moving exactly as predicted. In 1846 Urbain Le Verrier used Newton's law of gravity to calculate where an unknown planet must be pulling it. Johann Galle pointed a telescope at that spot on 23 September 1846 and found Neptune within about one degree of the prediction, on the first night of searching.` },
  { t: `The first measurement of the speed of light`, b: `In 1676 Ole Rømer noticed that the eclipses of Jupiter's moon Io came earlier when the Earth was nearer Jupiter and later when it was farther away. He realised light takes time to cross the Earth's orbit. The first estimate made from his data, by Christiaan Huygens, was about 220,000 km/s, not bad for the 1600s.` },
  { t: `The most famous failed experiment`, b: `In 1887 Albert Michelson and Edward Morley built an extremely sensitive instrument to detect the Earth moving through the "ether", the substance thought to carry light. They found nothing: light travelled at the same speed in every direction. This "null result" helped lead to Einstein's special relativity in 1905.` },
  { t: `The electron was the first particle found`, b: `In 1897 J. J. Thomson showed that cathode rays are made of tiny negatively charged particles, much lighter than any atom. It was the first subatomic particle ever discovered. Thirty years later his son, George Paget Thomson, showed that electrons also behave as waves, and both won Nobel Prizes.` },
  { t: `Measuring the charge of one electron with oil drops`, b: `Around 1909 Robert Millikan and Harvey Fletcher sprayed tiny oil droplets between two charged metal plates and adjusted the voltage until the drops floated. The charges on the drops always came in whole multiples of one tiny amount, the charge of one electron, about 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ coulombs.` },
  { t: `Planck's "act of desperation"`, b: `In 1900 Max Planck could only explain the colours of light from hot objects by assuming energy comes in tiny packets, or quanta, of size hf. He later called it "an act of desperation" and did not believe it at first. It was the birth of quantum physics, and the constant h is now used to define the kilogram.` },
  { t: `Einstein's Nobel Prize was not for relativity`, b: `Albert Einstein received the 1921 Nobel Prize in Physics "especially for his discovery of the law of the photoelectric effect", which showed that light comes in quanta (photons). Relativity was still controversial with the committee. The photoelectric effect is how solar panels and camera sensors work.` },
  { t: `A prince's thesis: matter is a wave`, b: `In his 1924 doctoral thesis, the French aristocrat Louis de Broglie proposed that electrons and all matter behave like waves, with wavelength h ÷ momentum. His examiners were unsure, so they sent it to Einstein, who liked it. Experiments confirmed electron waves in 1927, and electron microscopes now use them to see atoms.` },
  { t: `Dirac predicted antimatter from an equation`, b: `In 1928 Paul Dirac wrote an equation for the electron that combined quantum mechanics and relativity. It seemed to have extra solutions for a particle with the electron's mass but positive charge. In 1932 Carl Anderson found this "positron" in cosmic rays, the first antimatter particle.` },
  { t: `Cosmic rays were found in a balloon`, b: `In 1912 Victor Hess carried radiation detectors up to about 5,300 metres in a balloon, some flights at night and one during a solar eclipse. Radiation increased with height and did not depend on the Sun, so it had to come from space. These "cosmic rays" won him the 1936 Nobel Prize.` },
  { t: `The neutron completed the atom`, b: `Protons alone could not explain the masses of atoms. In 1932 James Chadwick discovered the neutron, a neutral particle in the nucleus with about the same mass as a proton. Because it has no charge, it can enter nuclei easily, which later made nuclear fission and reactors possible.` },
  { t: `Why the stars shine was solved in 1938`, b: `Until the 20th century nobody knew what powers the Sun. In 1938–39 Hans Bethe worked out the nuclear fusion reactions that turn hydrogen into helium inside stars, including the carbon–nitrogen–oxygen cycle. He won the 1967 Nobel Prize for explaining how stars make their energy.` },
  { t: `A limit for dying stars found on a ship`, b: `In 1930, sailing from India to England at age 19, Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar calculated that a white dwarf star cannot be heavier than about 1.4 times the Sun's mass, or it collapses. The famous astronomer Arthur Eddington publicly mocked the idea, but Chandrasekhar was right and won the 1983 Nobel Prize.` },
  { t: `Pulsars were first nicknamed "LGM"`, b: `In 1967 the graduate student Jocelyn Bell Burnell noticed a strange signal repeating every 1.34 seconds in her radio telescope data. It was so regular that it was jokingly labelled LGM-1, "Little Green Men". It was the first pulsar, a spinning neutron star. Her supervisor shared the 1974 Nobel Prize; she was not included.` },
  { t: `Henrietta Leavitt measured the universe`, b: `Around 1908–1912 Henrietta Swan Leavitt, working at Harvard counting stars on photographic plates, found that brighter pulsating Cepheid stars pulse more slowly. This relationship let astronomers measure distances to other galaxies. Edwin Hubble used it in the 1920s to show the universe is expanding.` },
  { t: `The universe's expansion is speeding up`, b: `In 1998 two teams studying distant exploding stars (supernovae) found they were fainter than expected: the expansion of the universe is accelerating. The cause, called "dark energy", makes up about 68% of the universe's energy, and no one knows what it is. The discovery won the 2011 Nobel Prize.` },
  { t: `Thousands of planets around other stars`, b: `The first planet around a Sun-like star, 51 Pegasi b, was found in 1995 by Michel Mayor and Didier Queloz, who noticed the star wobbling. Since then, especially with the Kepler space telescope, more than 6,000 exoplanets have been confirmed. Many are "hot Jupiters" or "super-Earths", types not found in our Solar System.` },
  { t: `Newton's cradle keeps the balls in line`, b: `In Newton's cradle, when one steel ball swings in and hits the row, one ball flies out the other end; if two swing in, two fly out. Both momentum and kinetic energy are conserved, and together these rules fix how many balls must leave. The toy is named after Newton but became popular only in the 1960s.` },
  { t: `Rockets push on themselves, not the air`, b: `Many people once thought rockets could not work in space because there is no air to push against. In 1920 the New York Times mocked Robert Goddard for this reason. But rockets work by Newton's third law: they push exhaust gas backwards and the gas pushes the rocket forwards. The newspaper printed a correction in 1969, as Apollo 11 flew to the Moon.` },
  { t: `Why curveballs curve`, b: `A spinning ball drags air around with it, so air flows faster on one side than the other. This creates a sideways force called the Magnus effect, described by Heinrich Magnus in 1852. It makes footballs bend in free kicks, tennis balls dip with topspin and table-tennis balls swerve.` },
  { t: `Golf balls have dimples to fly farther`, b: `A smooth golf ball would travel only about half as far as a dimpled one. The dimples make a thin layer of turbulent air cling to the ball longer, which shrinks the low-pressure wake behind it and reduces drag. Golfers discovered in the 1800s that old, scuffed balls flew better than new smooth ones.` },
  { t: `Why a boomerang comes back`, b: `A returning boomerang is shaped like two wings. As it spins, the arm moving forward moves faster through the air than the arm moving backward, so it gets more lift. This uneven force, combined with the gyroscopic effect of spinning, slowly turns the boomerang around in a circle back to the thrower.` },
  { t: `Why a kite flies`, b: `A kite held at an angle to the wind deflects air downwards. By Newton's third law the air pushes the kite up and back. The string holds it against the backward push, so it climbs. Kites have been flown in China for over 2,000 years, and in 1752 Benjamin Franklin is said to have used one to show that lightning is electricity.` },
  { t: `Why you feel pushed outwards in a turning car`, b: `When a car turns left, you feel thrown to the right. No force is actually pushing you outwards: your body tends to keep moving in a straight line (inertia), while the car turns underneath you. The seat and door then push you inwards to make you turn. This inward push is the centripetal force.` },
  { t: `Seat belts and crumple zones stretch out the crash`, b: `In a crash, the change in your momentum is fixed, but the force depends on how quickly it happens. Seat belts stretch slightly, airbags deflate, and crumple zones in the car's front collapse, all to make the stop last longer. A longer stopping time means a smaller force on your body: F = Δp ÷ Δt.` },
  { t: `Why you bend your knees when you land`, b: `Jumping from a wall onto stiff legs can hurt, but bending your knees makes the landing soft. The reason is the same as for airbags: bending increases the time over which your body stops, which reduces the force. Cats, athletes and parachutists all use this trick.` },
  { t: `Cats always land on their feet`, b: `A falling cat can turn itself right side up in mid-air without anything to push against. It does this by twisting its front and back halves in opposite directions, tucking in one half and stretching out the other. Physicists studied the "falling cat problem" with high-speed photographs as early as 1894.` },
  { t: `A falling raindrop stops speeding up`, b: `Raindrops fall from clouds hundreds or thousands of metres up, but they do not hit us at deadly speed. Air resistance grows with speed until it balances the drop's weight. A large raindrop reaches a terminal velocity of about 9 m/s. A skydiver in a belly-down position reaches about 55 m/s (200 km/h).` },
  { t: `Raindrops are not tear-shaped`, b: `Small raindrops are almost perfect spheres, held together by surface tension. Larger ones are flattened at the bottom by air resistance, looking more like a hamburger bun. Drops bigger than about 4–5 mm across usually break apart into smaller drops as they fall. The classic "teardrop" shape is a drawing convention.` },
  { t: `The shower curtain that attacks you`, b: `When you take a hot shower, the curtain often billows inwards against your legs. One explanation, from a 2001 computer simulation by David Schmidt, is that the spray creates a sideways swirling vortex with low pressure at its centre, which pulls the curtain in. Rising warm air and the Bernoulli effect probably help too.` },
  { t: `Why ketchup is hard to pour`, b: `Ketchup is a "shear-thinning" fluid: it stays thick when still but flows more easily once it is shaken or pushed. Tapping the side of the glass bottle near the neck, rather than the bottom, gives it the push it needs. Paint, blood and toothpaste also behave this way.` },
  { t: `You can run on cornflour and water`, b: `A thick mix of cornstarch and water ("oobleck") behaves like a liquid when you move slowly but becomes almost solid when you hit it hard. This is a shear-thickening fluid. People have run across pools of it, as long as they keep moving; if you stand still, you slowly sink.` },
  { t: `The longest-running lab experiment`, b: `In 1927 Professor Thomas Parnell at the University of Queensland poured heated pitch, a tar that seems solid enough to shatter with a hammer, into a funnel. Drops fall about once a decade: only nine have fallen so far. Nobody has ever seen one fall directly; the camera failed during the 2000 drop. Pitch is about 100 billion times more viscous than water.` },
  { t: `A metal lighter than water`, b: `Some metals float on water: lithium, sodium and potassium are all less dense than water, though they react with it. There are also engineered "metallic microlattices", made of tiny hollow metal tubes, that are about 99.99% air. One version developed in 2011 was light enough to rest on a dandelion seed head.` },
  { t: `Stretching rubber bands makes them warm`, b: `Stretch a thick rubber band quickly and touch it to your lip: it feels warm. Let it contract and it cools. Rubber is made of tangled chain molecules; stretching lines them up, which releases heat. This odd behaviour, studied by John Gough in 1805, is also why a hanging weight on a rubber band rises when the band is heated.` },
  { t: `Why wet clothes dry even on a cold day`, b: `Water does not need to boil to become vapour. At any temperature, the fastest molecules at the surface escape into the air. Wind carries the vapour away and lets more escape, which is why clothes dry faster on a windy day. Evaporation also takes away heat, which is why sweating cools you.` },
  { t: `Water boils at a lower temperature on mountains`, b: `At sea level water boils at 100 °C, but air pressure falls with height. On the summit of Mount Everest water boils at only about 70 °C, too cool to cook rice or noodles properly. That is why mountaineers use pressure cookers, which raise the boiling point instead.` },
  { t: `A drinking bird that seems to move forever`, b: `The "drinking bird" toy dips its beak into water, rises and dips again, again and again. It is not perpetual motion: water evaporating from its head cools it, lowering the vapour pressure of the liquid inside, so liquid rises into the head and tips the bird. It is a small heat engine powered by the room's warmth and dry air.` },
  { t: `Why the kettle sings before it boils`, b: `The rumbling noise a kettle makes before boiling comes from bubbles of steam forming at the hot bottom and then collapsing as they rise into cooler water. Each collapse makes a tiny bang. Once all the water is hot, the bubbles reach the surface without collapsing, and the kettle actually gets quieter.` },
  { t: `Snapping shrimp are louder than gunshots`, b: `The pistol shrimp snaps its claw shut so fast that it shoots out a jet of water, creating a bubble that collapses with a bang of over 200 decibels (measured in water) and a flash of light. For an instant the bubble reaches thousands of degrees. Colonies of snapping shrimp are one of the loudest sounds in the ocean.` },
  { t: `Whales talk across oceans`, b: `Sound travels far in water, and at a certain depth, around 1 km, it gets trapped in a layer called the SOFAR channel, where it can travel thousands of kilometres. Fin and blue whales make very low-frequency calls, and scientists think some can be heard hundreds of kilometres away.` },
  { t: `Elephants hear with their feet`, b: `Elephants make very low rumbles, some below 20 Hz, too low for humans to hear. These sounds also travel through the ground as vibrations. Research suggests elephants can detect them through sensitive nerve endings in their feet and trunks, possibly from kilometres away.` },
  { t: `Why your recorded voice sounds strange`, b: `When you speak, you hear your voice through the air and also through the bones of your skull, which carry low frequencies well. A recording only captures the sound through the air, so it sounds higher and thinner than the voice you are used to. Everyone else hears you like the recording.` },
  { t: `The Doppler effect was tested with a train of trumpeters`, b: `Christian Doppler predicted in 1842 that the pitch of a sound changes when the source moves towards or away from you. In 1845 Christophorus Buys Ballot tested it in the Netherlands by putting musicians on a moving train while others on the platform listened. Today the same effect is used in speed guns, weather radar and ultrasound scans of blood flow.` },
  { t: `Noise-cancelling headphones add sound`, b: `Noise-cancelling headphones use a microphone to listen to the sound around you, then play the opposite wave, with peaks where the noise has troughs. The two waves cancel out by destructive interference. It works best on steady low sounds such as an aeroplane engine.` },
  { t: `Why a straw looks bent in water`, b: `Light changes speed when it passes from water into air, so it bends. Your brain assumes light travels in straight lines, so the part of the straw under water appears in the wrong place and the straw looks broken at the surface. The same refraction makes swimming pools look shallower than they are.` },
  { t: `A mirage is light bending in hot air`, b: `On a hot day, a road ahead can seem to have puddles of water that vanish as you approach. Air just above the hot road is less dense, so light from the sky bends upwards as it passes through, reaching your eye from below. You are seeing an image of the sky on the road.` },
  { t: `Why diamonds sparkle`, b: `Diamond has a very high refractive index, about 2.42, so light inside it bends strongly and is easily trapped by total internal reflection. Jewellers cut diamonds so that light entering the top bounces around inside and comes back out the top. Diamond also splits white light into colours strongly, giving its "fire".` },
  { t: `Polarised sunglasses cut glare`, b: `Light reflected from water or a road is mostly polarised horizontally: its waves vibrate mainly side to side. Polarised sunglasses contain a filter that blocks horizontal vibrations, so they remove much of the glare while letting other light through. Bees and many other animals can see polarised light and use it to navigate.` },
  { t: `Butterfly wings are coloured without pigment`, b: `The brilliant blue of Morpho butterflies does not come from blue pigment. Their wing scales have tiny ridges, spaced at about the wavelength of light, that make blue light interfere constructively. This "structural colour" also makes peacock feathers, soap bubbles and the shimmer of CDs.` },
  { t: `You have a blind spot in each eye`, b: `Where the optic nerve leaves your eye, there are no light-sensitive cells, so each eye has a blind spot. You do not notice it because your brain fills in the gap using what is around it and what the other eye sees. You can find yours by closing one eye and moving a dot on paper until it disappears.` },
  { t: `Why distant mountains look blue`, b: `Air scatters blue light more than red, which is why the sky is blue. When you look at far-away mountains, there is so much air in between that scattered blue light is added to the view, and the mountains look bluish and paler. Painters since Leonardo da Vinci have used this "aerial perspective" to show distance.` },
  { t: `Why clouds are white and storm clouds grey`, b: `Cloud droplets are much larger than air molecules, so they scatter all colours of light roughly equally, and clouds look white. Very thick clouds let little light through from above, so their undersides look grey or dark. Rain clouds are not dirty; they are just in their own shadow.` },
  { t: `A spoon reflects you upside down`, b: `Look into the inside of a shiny spoon and your face is upside down; turn it over and you appear upright but small. The hollow side is a concave mirror, which forms an inverted image of things beyond its focal point. The back is a convex mirror, like the wide-angle mirrors used at road bends and in shops.` },
  { t: `Static shocks are thousands of volts`, b: `The small spark you feel when touching a door handle after walking on carpet can be several thousand volts, and a visible spark usually means at least about 3,000. It does not hurt you because the charge is tiny and flows for only a fraction of a microsecond. Dry air makes static worse, because moist air lets charge leak away.` },
  { t: `A lightning rod does not attract lightning from far away`, b: `Benjamin Franklin invented the lightning rod in the 1750s. It does not "attract" lightning from kilometres away; it gives a strike that would hit the building anyway a safe metal path to the ground. After some resistance, even churches adopted it, because church towers were being struck and bell-ringers killed.` },
  { t: `Electric eels deliver hundreds of volts`, b: `The electric eel of South America can produce shocks of up to about 600 volts (a species described in 2019 reached 860 volts), using thousands of modified muscle cells called electrocytes stacked like batteries. In 1800 Alessandro Volta was partly inspired by electric fish when he designed the first battery, which he compared to an "artificial electric organ".` },
  { t: `Your nerves send electric signals`, b: `Nerve signals are electrical, but they travel much more slowly than electricity in wires: from about 1 m/s up to about 120 m/s. They move by opening and closing channels that let sodium and potassium ions flow. Luigi Galvani discovered "animal electricity" in the 1780s by making dead frogs' legs twitch.` },
  { t: `Transformers are why power lines use high voltage`, b: `Power stations send electricity at hundreds of thousands of volts because, for the same power, a higher voltage means a lower current, and a lower current wastes far less energy as heat in the wires (loss = I²R). Transformers, invented in the 1880s, step the voltage up for travel and down again for homes.` },
  { t: `Induction stoves heat the pan, not the stove`, b: `An induction cooker has a coil carrying rapidly changing current. Its changing magnetic field induces electric currents in the bottom of an iron or steel pan, which heat the pan directly. The glass surface stays much cooler, and a glass or aluminium pan will not heat at all. It uses Faraday's law of induction, discovered in 1831.` },
  { t: `A magnet slows down in a copper pipe`, b: `Drop a strong magnet down a copper pipe and it falls surprisingly slowly, though copper is not magnetic. The moving magnet induces swirling "eddy currents" in the copper, which create their own magnetic field opposing the motion (Lenz's law). Roller coasters and some trains use the same effect as a smooth, frictionless brake.` },
  { t: `MRI magnets are tens of thousands of times stronger than Earth's`, b: `A hospital MRI scanner usually has a superconducting magnet of 1.5 or 3 tesla, tens of thousands of times stronger than the Earth's field. It makes hydrogen nuclei in your body line up, then radio waves knock them out of line; the signals they give off as they realign build a detailed picture of soft tissue, with no X-rays.` },
  { t: `A frog floated in a magnet`, b: `In 1997 Andre Geim and colleagues levitated a live frog in a very strong magnetic field of about 16 tesla. Water and most living tissue are weakly repelled by magnets (diamagnetism), and a strong enough field can balance gravity. The frog was unharmed. Geim won an Ig Nobel Prize for it and, in 2010, a real Nobel Prize for graphene.` },
  { t: `Birds may see the Earth's magnetic field`, b: `Many migrating birds, such as European robins, use the Earth's magnetic field to find their way. Research suggests that a protein in their eyes called cryptochrome reacts to magnetic fields through a quantum effect, so the birds may literally see the field as a pattern overlaid on their vision. Scientists are still studying how it works.` },
  { t: `Sunspots are cooler, not darker`, b: `Sunspots look black, but they are about 3,500–4,500 °C, compared with about 5,500 °C for the rest of the Sun's surface. Strong magnetic fields there block heat from rising. If you could see a sunspot on its own, it would shine brighter than a full Moon. Their number rises and falls in a cycle of about 11 years.` },
  { t: `The 1859 solar storm set telegraphs on fire`, b: `On 1 September 1859 Richard Carrington saw a brilliant flare on the Sun. Within a day a huge magnetic storm hit the Earth: auroras were seen near the tropics, and telegraph lines sparked, gave operators shocks and in some places worked without batteries. A similar storm today could damage satellites and power grids.` },
  { t: `Jupiter protects and threatens the inner planets`, b: `Jupiter's gravity is so strong that it has captured or deflected many comets. In July 1994 the world watched as pieces of comet Shoemaker–Levy 9 slammed into Jupiter, leaving dark scars larger than the Earth. It was the first collision between two Solar System bodies ever observed directly.` },
  { t: `Pushing an asteroid off course`, b: `In September 2022 NASA's DART spacecraft deliberately crashed into Dimorphos, a small asteroid moon orbiting a larger asteroid. The impact shortened its orbit by about 32 minutes, far more than the minimum goal of 73 seconds. It was the first test of whether we could deflect an asteroid heading for Earth.` },
  { t: `Footprints on the Moon will last millions of years`, b: `The Moon has no wind or rain, so the footprints left by Apollo astronauts between 1969 and 1972 should last for millions of years, slowly worn away only by tiny meteorite impacts. The flags they planted, however, have probably been bleached white by unfiltered sunlight.` }
];
